# ⛔ Line endings, decided once here rather than rediscovered by a tool.
#
# Measured 2026-10-01: a CRLF after `case $link in` makes `dash` fail with
# `Syntax error: word unexpected (expecting "in")` while `bash -n` accepts the
# same file. It has now happened three times in this repository, and once
# because an edit rewrote a file with a Python script that wrote text mode.
# ⛔ `scripts/check-scripts.py` checks the shell scripts, and the CI workflow
# runs it; this file is what stops the conversion happening in the first place.
#
# ⛔ `* text=auto` would have converted `check-todo.py` on checkout, which is a
# Python file this repository runs directly. The shell scripts are pinned to LF
# below rather than left to normalisation, because the failure mode is a shell
# that parses in one shell and not the other.

* text=auto

*.sh text eol=lf
scripts/gate.sh text eol=lf
scripts/plant.sh text eol=lf
scripts/dev.sh text eol=lf

*.py text eol=lf
*.rs text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.lock text eol=lf
LICENSE text eol=lf

# ⛔ **`*.txt` is pinned for one file, and the file is a contract.**
# `crates/podssh-probe/tests/spec/relay-spec-2026-10-03-r2.txt` is the pinned
# copy of the relay's document, and two of its tests assert its SHA-256 and its
# byte count. ⛔ MEASURED 2026-10-05: with `text=auto` and no `eol`, a clone on a
# machine using git's defaults (native eol on Windows, or `core.autocrlf=true`)
# checks that file out CRLF, and both assertions fail on a tree nothing is wrong
# with — a check that fires on correct input. `*.patch` is here for the same
# reason: a patch set applied with `git apply` must survive a checkout.
*.txt text eol=lf
*.patch text eol=lf

# IRC wire fixtures are protocol bytes: CRLF is IRC's line terminator and
# `crates/podssh-core/tests/common/mod.rs` asserts it is present. `text eol=lf`
# above silently rewrote it to LF on every checkout, so CI failed while a
# Windows working tree (still CRLF) passed. Store these byte-for-byte.
crates/podssh-core/tests/fixtures/*.txt -text

# ⛔ A binary is a binary, and `text=auto` guesses from content.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gz binary

# RSA signature vectors made by OpenSSL: bytes, never text.
crates/podssh-ws/tests/fixtures/rsa/*.der binary
crates/podssh-ws/tests/fixtures/rsa/*.sig binary
crates/podssh-ws/tests/fixtures/rsa/msg -text
